In `poll()`, branch on status before the file check:
```ts
const st = await reportBuilderApi.executionStatus(id);
if (st.status === 'FAILED') {
  stopPolling();
  setError('执行失败（任务已终止，请查看执行记录）');
  setPhase('failed');
  return;
}
if (st.report_file_id) { ...existing done path... }
```
Assume 'FAILED' is the terminal failure literal; confirm against `p8_007_execution_log_status` and `ReportExecutionLog.status` before applying.

Same generation-ref pattern used for the poller, applied here:

const defGen = useRef(0);
useEffect(() => {
  if (!selected) { setDef(null); return; }
  const my = ++defGen.current;
  void (async () => {
    try {
      const d = await reportBuilderApi.get(selected);
      if (defGen.current === my) setDef(d);
    } catch (err) {
      if (defGen.current === my) setError(errorMessage(err));
    }
  })();
  return () => { defGen.current += 1; };
}, [selected]);

Two-part, both small. (1) Make the premise executable rather than asserted — before the drop loop, refuse to proceed on a non-empty legacy table, e.g. add a guard that raises with the offending row counts so a deploy against a populated database fails loudly instead of silently:

```python
def upgrade() -> None:
    for t in _DROP_ORDER:
        conn = op.get_bind()
        n = conn.execute(sa.text(f"SELECT count(*) FROM {t}")).scalar() if _exists(conn, t) else 0
        if n:
            raise RuntimeError(f"p8_001 拒绝 drop 非空表 {t}（{n} 行）—— 先导出再重跑")
    for t in _DROP_ORDER:
        op.drop_table(t, if_exists=True)
```

(2) Before running the upgrade against any non-fresh database, capture the legacy rows so the drop is recoverable:

```sql
-- read-only, run BEFORE `alembic upgrade p8_001`
SELECT count(*) AS report_definitions_rows FROM report_definitions;
SELECT count(*) AS report_execution_logs_rows FROM report_execution_logs;
\copy (SELECT * FROM report_definitions) TO '/tmp/p8_pre_report_definitions.csv' CSV HEADER
\copy (SELECT * FROM report_execution_logs) TO '/tmp/p8_pre_report_execution_logs.csv' CSV HEADER
```

Note that `pcs_test` is a known-stale second database (CLAUDE.md records it lagging revisions); if this migration is ever pointed at it, the same guard is what will catch the drift.
